## Common Recovery Commands

### Check backup status

```bash
# List available backup sets and dates
duply <profile-name> status

# List files in the latest backup
duply <profile-name> list

# List files as of a specific date
duply <profile-name> list 3D          # 3 days ago
duply <profile-name> list 2026-05-28  # specific date
```

### Restore entire backup

```bash
# Restore latest state to a temporary directory (SAFE — does not overwrite source)
duply <profile-name> restore /tmp/restore-test

# Restore to a specific point in time
duply <profile-name> restore /tmp/restore-test 3D
duply <profile-name> restore /tmp/restore-test 2026-05-28T09:00:00

# Restore directly to original location (DESTRUCTIVE — overwrites current files)
duply <profile-name> restore <source-path>
```

### Restore a single file

```bash
# Fetch one file from the latest backup
duply <profile-name> fetch path/to/file.php /tmp/recovered-file.php

# Fetch from a specific date
duply <profile-name> fetch path/to/file.php /tmp/recovered-file.php 2D
```

Note: paths in `fetch` are relative to the SOURCE directory.

### Verify backup integrity

```bash
# Compare latest backup against current source (shows differences)
duply <profile-name> verify
```

### Manual backup (outside cron schedule)

```bash
# Run an incremental backup now
duply <profile-name> incr

# Force a full backup now
duply <profile-name> full

# Full backup + verify + purge old sets
duply <profile-name> full+verify+purgeFull --force
```

## Recovery Workflow (Full Site Restore)

1. **Stop services** to prevent writes during restore:
   ```bash
   sudo systemctl stop httpd varnish php-fpm lsyncd@*
   ```

2. **Restore files** to a temp location first:
   ```bash
   sudo duply <profile-name> restore /tmp/site-restore
   ```

3. **Verify** the restored content looks correct:
   ```bash
   ls -la /tmp/site-restore/
   diff -r /tmp/site-restore/wp-config.php <source>/wp-config.php
   ```

4. **Swap into place** (or rsync selectively):
   ```bash
   sudo rsync -av --delete /tmp/site-restore/ <source>/
   ```

5. **Restore database** (if DB dump was included in backup):
   ```bash
   gunzip -c <source>/secure/<profile>-db-backup.sql.gz | wp db import - --path=<source>
   # or with mysql client:
   gunzip -c <source>/secure/<profile>-db-backup.sql.gz | mysql -u <user> -p <dbname>
   ```

6. **Restart services:**
   ```bash
   sudo systemctl start httpd varnish php-fpm lsyncd@*
   ```

## Profile Configs

Profile configurations are stored in `/etc/duply/<profile-name>/conf`.

## Troubleshooting

```bash
# Run backup with maximum verbosity
duply <profile-name> incr -- --verbosity 9

# Check what duplicity sees in the target
duplicity collection-status <target-url>

# Force full backup if incremental chain is broken
duply <profile-name> full
```
